BEGIN:VEVENT
IMAGE;VALUE=URI;DISPLAY=BADGE;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://architecturehousto
 n.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/AIA_Approved_CE_horiz_2c-1200x494-4e6c487
 .jpg
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://architecturehouston.org/wp-content/upload
 s/2025/02/AIA_Approved_CE_horiz_2c-1200x494-4e6c487.jpg
UID:HwH0HISrQFNoK0CwUlRA2
SUMMARY:Full Day Continuing Education
DTSTAMP:20260819T033133Z
DTSTART:20260825T090000Z
DESCRIPTION:Urbanism Continuum CE day\n  \n9:00-9:30am Breakfast and Regist
	ration \n9:30-10:45am / Presentation\nBuilding Bridges: Collaborative Plac
	emaking in the Bayou City – 1.25 AIA HSW\nPresented by Michael Robinson\, 
	PLA\, ASLA\, Principal\, SWA Group \nMichael is a landscape architect focu
	sed on the intersection of infrastructure and public space. He leads the d
	esign and implementation of complex park\, trail\, and civic landscape pro
	jects\, connecting regional systems into cohesive\, accessible environment
	s. His work spans planning through realization\, coordinating multidiscipl
	inary teams and public agencies to deliver resilient\, buildable solutions
	. With a background in both architecture and landscape architecture\, he b
	rings a systems-based approach that integrates environmental performance\,
	 mobility\, and user experience into high-performing public landscapes. \n
	11:00-12:00pm / Presentation \nUniversal Design: Building Accessible and H
	ealthy Spaces – 1.00 AIA HSW (Barrier Free)\nPresented by Kyle J. Mutz\, D
	irector\, University of Houston / Justin Dart Jr. Student Accessibility Ce
	nter \nKyle is the Director of the Justin Dart Jr. Student Accessibility C
	enter at the University of Houston\, where he champions accessibility\, in
	clusion\, and student success. Drawing from both his professional expertis
	e and lived experience growing up with a disability\, Kyle is committed to
	 creating equitable opportunities for all. A former U.S. wheelchair racing
	 athlete\, he brings a unique perspective on disability advocacy and is de
	dicated to advancing the legacy of Justin Dart Jr. through education and e
	mpowerment. \n  \n12:00-12:30pm – Break for Lunch  \n  \n12:30-1:45pm / Pr
	esentation \nFrom Paint to Policy: How Tactical Urbanism Became a Capital 
	Project – 1.25 AIA HSW & 1.25 LA/CES HSW\nPresented by Ed Pettitt\, MPH – 
	President of Columbia Tap and Urban Planner with Asakura Robinson \nEd is 
	an urban planner and One Health specialist whose academic path spans human
	 biology at Cornell University\, global health and healthcare management a
	t the University of Texas School of Public Health\, and doctoral studies i
	n Urban Planning and Environmental Policy at Texas Southern University. In
	 addition\, Ed is also the President of the Columbia Tap Trail.  He provid
	es organizational leadership by guiding the board\, strengthening partners
	hips\, advancing strategic initiatives\, and overseeing programs that supp
	ort equitable community development along the Columbia Tap Trail. \n2:00-3
	:15pm / Presentation + Tour\nReclaiming Main Street: Designing a Lasting L
	egacy in the Public Realm  – 1.25 AIA HSW & 1.25 LA/CES HSW\nPresented by 
	Alex Ramirez\, PLA & Sarah Smith\, PLA\, CDT\, Design Workshop \nAlex is a
	 Principal in the Houston studio at Design Workshop\, with expertise as bo
	th a landscape architect and urban designer. His diverse experience spans 
	across the private and public sectors\, involving every phase of the desig
	n process—from conceptual planning and site analysis to construction docum
	entation and detailed observation. Alex is dedicated to delivering top-tie
	r work by crafting innovative design solutions for plazas\, streetscapes\,
	 parks\, and open spaces. His approach integrates social\, cultural\, econ
	omic\, and environmental considerations\, ensuring that each project enhan
	ces the surrounding neighborhoods and communities in meaningful ways. \nSa
	rah is a landscape architect in our Houston studio with a passion for the 
	outdoors\, thoughtful design\, and creating meaningful places. From concep
	t through construction\, she brings creativity\, attention to detail\, and
	 a collaborative approach to projects ranging from parks and corporate cam
	puses to urban streetscapes. Sarah earned her Bachelor of Landscape Archit
	ecture from Louisiana State University and is an active member of ULI\, Ce
	ntral Houston\, and ASLA. \n  \nContinuing Education Credits: \n\nAIA = 4.
	75 HSW\nLA/CES = 3.75 HSW\n\n  \n\nIndividual classes are not available fo
	r the event.\nPre-registered pricing includes breakfast and lunch.\nParkin
	g is not included.\nA minimum of 3 business days’ notice is required to re
	ceive a full refund if you need to cancel your reservation.\nOnline regist
	ration will close Thursday\, August 20\nOnsite registration is available f
	or day of (breakfast and lunch is NOT included)\n\nhttps://architecturehou
	ston.org/event/full-day-continuing-education-8/\n\nFrom AIA Houston
URL:https://architecturehouston.org/event/full-day-continuing-education-8/
LOCATION:Architecture Center Houston\, 902 Commerce St.\, Houston\, TX\, 77
	002\, United States
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:aia-houston
CATEGORIES:AIA Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT7H
END:VEVENT
